RC-WR02 log: failed first install from the DMG (installer re-verify fails), no installed copy; post-fix 1.8.1 rig build of 83df1e1f
--- notes.txt
Method: a first install from the DMG in ~/Downloads (release asset name, unquarantined), with no
installed copy. A VM-side watcher wrote "off" to ~/p18/verify-mode as soon as the installer process
existed (07:41:43.284). The DMG GUI runs all its checks before it spawns the installer (VerifyApp,
CheckStagedApp, and the installer copy's copy-check), so those checks ran with "adhoc". The installer's
re-verify of the staged copy then ran the real codesign against this ad-hoc build and failed with
app-team. That is the re-verify branch of RunInstaller, and it reaches finishOpen and revealLeft with no
copy installed. The F4 approach (a mismatched bundle version) would fail CheckStagedApp in the DMG GUI,
before any installer runs, and never reach revealLeft, so it was not used.

What the user sees: the DMG GUI's window, then no Portty window (the GUI quit after the installer
started; the poller shows no Portty GUI from 07:41:43.7). No Portty alert or dialog. At 07:41:55.6 a
Finder window "Downloads" comes to the front with portty-v1.8.1-darwin-arm64.dmg selected (screenshot,
and Finder AX: AXWindow Title=Downloads, the DMG's name field). The volume is ejected. Nothing explains
why, unless the user reads update.log.
(The Finder "Portty" volume window stayed until the eject at about +12 s; in R4-a the eject took 1.5 s.
No eject failure was logged.)

Next manual launch: the user drags Portty from the DMG into /Applications (ditto from the remounted
volume) and opens it. The installed 1.8.1 takes the result:
"update: result failed  to 1.8.1 reverify" (the old version is empty), then
"update: result not shown: not newer than this version". No banner and no notification appear, as the
WR-02 fix designed for a copy installed by hand at the same version.
Not run: opening the DMG's app again instead. That starts mover mode, which never reads the result
(startMover returns before consumeInstallResult). Its installer would then overwrite the failed result,
so the user would never see it.
